.TH "udpsvd" "8" 
.SH NAME
udpsvd \- UDP/IP service daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]udpsvd\f[R] [\-hpvv] [\-u \f[I]user\f[R]] [\-l \f[I]name\f[R]] [\-i
\f[I]dir\f[R]|\-x \f[I]cdb\f[R]] [\-t \f[I]sec\f[R]] \f[I]host\f[R]
\f[I]port\f[R] \f[I]prog\f[R]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]udpsvd\f[R] creates an UDP/IP socket, binds it to the address
\f[I]host\f[R]:\f[I]port\f[R], and listens on the socket for incoming
datagrams.
.PP
If a datagram is available on the socket, \f[B]udpsvd\f[R] conditionally
starts a program, with standard input reading from the socket, and
standard output redirected to standard error, to handle this, and
possibly more datagrams.
\f[B]udpsvd\f[R] does not start the program if another program that it
has started before still is running.
If the program exits, \f[B]udpsvd\f[R] again listens to the socket until
a new datagram is available.
If there are still datagrams available on the socket, the program is
restarted immediately.
.PP
\f[B]udpsvd\f[R] optionally checks for special intructions depending on
the IP address or hostname of the client sending the datagram which not
yet was handled by a running program, see \f[B]ipsvd\-instruct\f[R](5)
for details.
.SS ATTENTION:
UDP is a connectionless protocol.
Most programs that handle user datagrams, such as \f[B]talkd\f[R](8),
keep running after receiving a datagram, and process subsequent
datagrams sent to the socket until a timeout is reached.
\f[B]udpsvd\f[R] only checks special instructions for a datagram that
causes a startup of the program; not if a program handling datagrams
already is running.
It doesn\[aq]t make much sense to restrict access through special
instructions when using such a program.
.PP
On the other hand, it makes perfectly sense with programs like
\f[B]tftpd\f[R](8), that fork to establish a separate connection to the
client when receiving the datagram.
In general it\[aq]s adequate to set up special instructions for programs
that support being run by tcpwrapper.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\f[I]host\f[R]
\f[I]host\f[R] either is a hostname, or a dotted\-decimal IP address, or
0.
If \f[I]host\f[R] is 0, \f[B]udpsvd\f[R] accepts datagrams to any local
IP address.
.TP
\f[I]port\f[R]
\f[B]udpsvd\f[R] accepts datagrams to \f[I]host\f[R]:\f[I]port\f[R].
\f[I]port\f[R] may be a name from /etc/services or a number.
.TP
\f[I]prog\f[R]
\f[I]prog\f[R] consists of one or more arguments.
\f[B]udpsvd\f[R] normally runs \f[I]prog\f[R] to handle a datagram, and
possibly more, that is sent to the socket, if there is no program that
was started before by \f[B]udpsvd\f[R] still running and handling
datagrams.
.TP
\f[B]\-i\f[R] \f[I]dir\f[R]
read instructions for handling new connections from the instructions
directory \f[I]dir\f[R].
See \f[B]ipsvd\-instruct\f[R](5) for details.
.TP
\f[B]\-x\f[R] \f[I]cdb\f[R]
read instructions for handling new connections from the constant
database \f[I]cdb\f[R].
The constant database normally is created from an instructions directory
by running \f[B]ipsvd\-cdb\f[R](8).
.TP
\f[B]\-t\f[R] \f[I]sec\f[R]
timeout.
This option only takes effect if the \-i option is given.
While checking the instructions directory, check the time of last access
of the file that matches the clients address or hostname if any, discard
and remove the file if it wasn\[aq]t accessed within the last
\f[I]sec\f[R] seconds; \f[B]udpsvd\f[R] does not discard or remove a
file if the user\[aq]s write permission is not set, for those files the
timeout is disabled.
Default is 0, which means that the timeout is disabled.
.TP
\f[B]\-l\f[R] \f[I]name\f[R]
local hostname.
Do not look up the local hostname in DNS, but use \f[I]name\f[R] as
hostname.
By default \f[B]udpsvd\f[R] looks up the local hostname once at startup.
.TP
\f[B]\-u\f[R] [:]\f[I]user\f[R][:\f[I]group\f[R]]
drop permissions.
Set uid and gid to the \f[I]user\f[R]\[aq]s uid and gid, as found in
\f[I]/etc/passwd\f[R], before running \f[I]prog\f[R].
If \f[I]user\f[R] is followed by a colon and a \f[I]group\f[R], set the
gid to \f[I]group\f[R]\[aq]s gid, as found in \f[I]/etc/group\f[R],
instead of \f[I]user\f[R]\[aq]s gid.
If \f[I]group\f[R] consists of a colon\-separated list of group names,
set the group ids of all listed groups.
If \f[I]user\f[R] is prefixed with a colon, the \f[I]user\f[R] and all
\f[I]group\f[R] arguments are interpreted as uid and gids respectively,
and not looked up in the password or group file.
All supplementary groups are removed.
.TP
\f[B]\-h\f[R]
Look up the client\[aq]s hostname in DNS.
.TP
\f[B]\-p\f[R]
paranoid.
After looking up the client\[aq]s hostname in DNS, look up the IP
addresses in DNS for that hostname, and forget the hostname if none of
the addresses match the client\[aq]s IP address.
You should set this option if you use hostname based instructions.
The \-p option implies the \-h option.
.TP
\f[B]\-v\f[R]
verbose.
Print verbose messages to standard output.
.TP
\f[B]\-vv\f[R]
more verbose.
Print more verbose messages to standard output.
